TEMPORARY diagnostic instrumentation for the cold-launch extension host OOM's ~35-second blocking window -- not a real fix, remove once root-caused.

ios-exthost-worker-heap.diff's own "UPDATE 4" comment documents a real,
captured ~35-second gap with zero diagnostic output (the event loop fully
blocked) between a heap-stats reading and the eventual
ERR_WORKER_OUT_OF_MEMORY crash on a cold launch specifically. WHAT runs
during that window is still unknown.

vscode's own LogService already logs exactly this
("ExtensionService#_doActivateExtension <id>, startup: ..., activationEvent: ...")
for every single extension activation, in AbstractExtHostExtensionService
-- but confirmed (by capturing this app's entire on-disk container tree
in a real simulator-test.yml run and finding no exthost-related log file
anywhere) that this LogService line never reaches any artifact this
project's CI can see. Only the agent host's own separate "ahp" JSONL
protocol logs are written to disk under
vscode-server-data/data/logs/<timestamp>/ -- a real, useful discovery in
its own right, but a dead end for finding out what the extension host
itself is doing.

Adds console.error() calls directly around the activation Promise chain
in _doActivateExtension instead: start, "loaded, calling activate()", and
done, each with an elapsed-ms timestamp and the extension's identifier.
console.error (not process.stderr.write) specifically because this file
(src/vs/workbench/api/common/extHostExtensionService.ts) is shared
between the browser and Node extension host contexts -- console.error's
type is universal, avoiding the DOM-vs-Node ambient-type problem
ios-exthost-heap-diagnostic.diff already hit and fixed once for
setInterval/.unref(). Node's console implementation writes to the real
process.stderr file descriptor regardless of which console.* method is
called, and that IS already piped into the parent's captured
node-stdio.log by extensionHostConnection.ts's existing "<worker><stderr>"
forwarding -- already confirmed working for the unrelated "WebAssembly is
not defined" promise-rejection message seen in every previously-captured
log.

Purely additive, no behavior change to activation itself -- only new
console.error calls. Meant to be removed (or converted into a real fix)
once a captured log shows which extension's activation is the one that
blocks for ~35 seconds on a cold launch.

